arXiv:2510.05703v1 [cs.LG] 07 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimization
<br class="ltx_break">for Constrained LLM Alignment</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yihan Du
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>SUTD ESD
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:yihan_du@sutd.edu.sg">yihan_du@sutd.edu.sg</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Seo Taek Kong
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>UIUC ECE
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:skong10@illinois.edu">skong10@illinois.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">R. Srikant
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>UIUC ECE
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:rsrikant@illinois.edu">rsrikant@illinois.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The widespread application of Large Language Models (LLMs) imposes increasing demands on safety, such as reducing harmful content and fake information, and avoiding certain forbidden tokens due to rules and laws. While there have been several recent works studying safe alignment of LLMs, these works either require the training of reward and cost models and incur high memory and computational costs, or need prior knowledge about the optimal solution. Motivated by this fact, we study the problem of constrained alignment in LLMs, i.e., maximizing the output reward while restricting the cost due to potentially unsafe content to stay below a threshold. For this problem, we propose a novel primal-dual DPO approach, which first trains a model using standard DPO on reward preference data to provide reward information, and then adopts a rearranged Lagrangian DPO objective utilizing the provided reward information to fine-tune LLMs on cost preference data. Our approach significantly reduces memory and computational costs, and does not require extra prior knowledge. Moreover, we establish rigorous theoretical guarantees on the suboptimality and constraint violation of the output policy. We also extend our approach to an online data setting by incorporating exploration bonuses, which enables our approach to explore uncovered prompt-response space, and then provide theoretical results that get rid of the dependence on preference data coverage. Experimental results on the widely-used preference dataset PKU-SafeRLHF demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al.(2023)Achiam, Adler, Agarwal, Ahmad, Akkaya, Aleman,
Almeida, Altenschmidt, Altman, Anadkat, et al.</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al.(2023a)Touvron, Lavril, Izacard, Martinet,
Lachaux, Lacroix, Rozière, Goyal, Hambro, Azhar,
et al.</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al.(2023b)Touvron, Martin, Stone, Albert,
Almahairi, Babaei, Bashlykov, Batra, Bhargava, Bhosale,
et al.</a>]</cite> have achieved a remarkable success in dialogues, summarization, instruction following, etc. Despite the huge success of LLMs, LLMs may also output fabricated information and harmful content, such as texts involving discrimination, crimes and moral issues <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gehman et al.(2020)Gehman, Gururangan, Sap, Choi, and
Smith</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al.(2021)Lin, Hilton, and Evans</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al.(2023)Wei, Haghtalab, and Steinhardt</a>]</cite>. With the extensive application of LLMs, how to align them to enhance safety or impose constraints has become a crucial problem. For example, we want to prevent LLMs from generating content that may have negative societal impacts or ethical concerns. In Agentic AI or AI education applications, we need to avoid certain tokens due to some rules and laws, or course content that has not been taught.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, there are several works studying the safety alignment of LLMs. A popular formulation is the <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">constrained alignment problem</em>, which aims to maximize the reward while constraining the cost to stay below a threshold. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>]</cite> proposed a safe reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) framework for this problem, which train reward and cost models on reward and cost preference data, respectively, and then apply an RL algorithm to fine-tune LLMs to maximize the Lagrangian function under the learned reward and cost functions.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al.(2024b)Liu, Sun, and Zheng</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wachi et al.(2024)Wachi, Tran, Sato, Tanabe, and
Akimoto</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al.(2024)Huang, Li, Dobriban, Bastani, Hassani, and
Ding</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al.(2025)Kim, Jang, Kim, Kim, Lee, Bae, and
Lee</a>]</cite> designed direct preference optimization (DPO)-based safety alignment approaches. The idea of DPO is to directly fine-tune LLMs using preference data, without training a reward model. However, these works either still require trained reward and cost models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al.(2024b)Liu, Sun, and Zheng</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al.(2024)Huang, Li, Dobriban, Bastani, Hassani, and
Ding</a>]</cite>, or need prior knowledge of the optimal Lagrange multiplier <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wachi et al.(2024)Wachi, Tran, Sato, Tanabe, and
Akimoto</a>]</cite>, or are inefficient in cost information learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al.(2025)Kim, Jang, Kim, Kim, Lee, Bae, and
Lee</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by the above facts, we propose a novel and provably efficient primal-dual DPO approach. Our approach first trains a model using standard DPO on reward preference data, and then fine-tunes LLMs with a rearranged Lagrangian DPO objective on cost preference data, utilizing the reward information provided by the standard DPO-trained model. Our approach avoids training reward and cost models, which significantly reduces memory and computational costs, and does not require any prior knowledge about the optimal solution. Moreover, we establish rigorous theoretical guarantees on the suboptimality and constraint violation of the output policy. Finally, we investigate an online setting where collecting preference data online is allowed. In this setting, we adopt exploration bonuses in our primal-dual DPO approach to guide exploration on uncovered prompt-response space, and provide theoretical results that remove the dependence on preference data coverage. All proofs are deferred to Appendix due to space limits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The contributions of our work are summarized as follows.</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a novel primal-dual DPO approach for the constrained alignment problem of LLMs. This approach first trains a model using standard DPO on reward preference data to offer reward information, and then adopts a rearranged Lagrangian DPO objective to fine-tune LLMs utilizing the offered reward information. This approach neither requires to train reward and cost models, which significantly saves memory and computational costs, nor needs prior knowledge about the optimal solution.
We also provide rigorous suboptimality and cost violation guarantees.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct experiments on the PKU-SafeRLHF preference dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>]</cite> with model-based and GPT-4 evaluations. Empirical results show that our approach achieves an effective helpfulness-harmlessness trade-off without training reward and cost models.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the online data setting, by incorporating exploration bonuses in our rearranged DPO objective, our approach can effectively explore uncovered prompt-response space, and enjoys theoretical results that get rid of the dependence on preference data coverage.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we review the most related work and defer a more detailed description to Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A A Full Review of Related Work ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With the rapid development of LLMs, the alignment of LLMs has received extensive attention.
RLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al.(2017)Christiano, Leike, Brown, Martic, Legg, and
Amodei</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al.(2022)Ouyang, Wu, Jiang, Almeida, Wainwright, Mishkin,
Zhang, Agarwal, Slama, Ray, et al.</a>]</cite> and DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al.(2023)Rafailov, Sharma, Mitchell, Manning, Ermon, and
Finn</a>]</cite> are the two main algorithmic frameworks for LLM alignment. RLHF first trains a reward model, and then applies an RL algorithm with the learned reward model to fine-tune LLMs. DPO does not explicitly train a reward model, but instead directly fine-tunes LLMs using preference data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, to reduce the harmful content generation of LLMs, there are several works studying safety alignment.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>]</cite> proposed a safe RLHF framework. This framework trains a reward model and a cost model on reward and cost preference data, respectively, and then applies an RL algorithm, PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al.(2017)Schulman, Wolski, Dhariwal, Radford, and
Klimov</a>]</cite>, to maximize the Lagrangian function using the learned reward and cost functions.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al.(2024b)Liu, Sun, and Zheng</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al.(2024)Huang, Li, Dobriban, Bastani, Hassani, and
Ding</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al.(2025)Zhang, Li, Hounie, Bastani, Ding, and
Ribeiro</a>]</cite> used trained reward and cost models to regenerate preference data according to the Lagrangian function, and then performed DPO on regenerated data. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wachi et al.(2024)Wachi, Tran, Sato, Tanabe, and
Akimoto</a>]</cite> observed a relationship between the optimal solution of maximizing the Lagrangian function and that of maximizing the reward function, and designed a stepwise DPO approach leveraging this observation. However, their approach requires prior knowledge of the optimal Lagrange multiplier, and their theoretical results depend on the deviation between the used and optimal Lagrange multipliers, which can be unbounded. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al.(2025)Kim, Jang, Kim, Kim, Lee, Bae, and
Lee</a>]</cite> reordered preference data if the preferred response
is unsafe and the not-preferred response is safe,
and ran DPO on reordered data. Their approach is inefficient in cost information learning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to the above works, our approach does not require trained reward and cost models, which saves memory and computational costs, or prior knowledge of the optimal Lagrange multiplier. Moreover, we investigate the online data setting, and develop an exploratory primal-dual DPO approach which achieves theoretical results without the dependence on preference data coverage.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF).</span>
The RLHF framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al.(2017)Christiano, Leike, Brown, Martic, Legg, and
Amodei</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al.(2022)Ouyang, Wu, Jiang, Almeida, Wainwright, Mishkin,
Zhang, Agarwal, Slama, Ray, et al.</a>]</cite> consists of three phases: (i) supervised fine-tuning a pre-trained LLM on a high-quality dataset of downstream tasks, e.g., dialogue and summarization, (ii) reward model learning, and (iii) RL optimization with the learned reward model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the sets of all possible prompts and responses. We define a policy <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\triangle_{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\triangle_{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a mapping from <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to a distribution on <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangle_{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangle_{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of all distributions on <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>. We formulate an LLM as a policy, and use <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\textup{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\textup{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the supervised fine-tuned (SFT) model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the reward model learning phase, we have access to a reward preference dataset <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{r}=\{x^{r}_{i},y^{rw}_{i},y^{r\ell}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N^{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{r}=\{x^{r}_{i},y^{rw}_{i},y^{r\ell}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N^{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a prompt, <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{rw}_{i},y^{r\ell}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{rw}_{i},y^{r\ell}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are preferred and dispreferred responses under prompt <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the superscript <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> refers to reward preference. The generation of preference data is as follows: We assume that there exists an <em id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unknown</em> reward function <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}(x,y)\in[-R_{\max},R_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}(x,y)\in[-R_{\max},R_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math> for some constant <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>, which models the helpfulness of response <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> under prompt <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Human annotators compare a pair of responses <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{rw},y^{r\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{rw},y^{r\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> under prompt <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we assume that the probability that <math id="S3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{rw}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{rw}</annotation></semantics></math> is preferred to <math id="S3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{r\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{r\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> under prompt <math id="S3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> follows the Bradley-Terry model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bradley &amp; Terry(1952)Bradley and Terry</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Pr\left[y^{rw}\succ y^{r\ell}|x\right]=\frac{\exp(r^{*}(x,y^{rw}))}{\exp(r^{*}(x,y^{rw}))+\exp(r^{*}(x,y^{r\ell}))}=\sigma\left(r^{*}(x,y^{rw})-r^{*}(x,y^{r\ell})\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≻</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Pr\left[y^{rw}\succ y^{r\ell}|x\right]=\frac{\exp(r^{*}(x,y^{rw}))}{\exp(r^{*}(x,y^{rw}))+\exp(r^{*}(x,y^{r\ell}))}=\sigma\left(r^{*}(x,y^{rw})-r^{*}(x,y^{r\ell})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(z):=\frac{1}{1+\exp(-z)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(z):=\frac{1}{1+\exp(-z)}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sigmoid function.
With the preference data, we train a reward model <math id="S3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> via maximum likelihood estimation (MLE), i.e., minimizing the negative log-likelihood loss:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{r}\ -\frac{1}{N^{r}}\sum_{i=1}^{N^{r}}\log\sigma\left(r(x^{r}_{i},y^{rw}_{i})-r(x^{r}_{i},y^{r\ell}_{i})\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>r</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.500em">−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{r}\ -\frac{1}{N^{r}}\sum_{i=1}^{N^{r}}\log\sigma\left(r(x^{r}_{i},y^{rw}_{i})-r(x^{r}_{i},y^{r\ell}_{i})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the RL optimization phase, we apply RL algorithms, e.g., PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al.(2017)Schulman, Wolski, Dhariwal, Radford, and
Klimov</a>]</cite>, to fine-tune the SFT model under the learned reward model <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\pi}\ \mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}}\left[\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi(\cdot|x)}\left[r(x,y)\right]-\beta\cdot\textup{KL}\left(\pi(\cdot|x)\|\pi_{\textup{ref}}(\cdot|x)\right)\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\pi}\ \mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}}\left[\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi(\cdot|x)}\left[r(x,y)\right]-\beta\cdot\textup{KL}\left(\pi(\cdot|x)\|\pi_{\textup{ref}}(\cdot|x)\right)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is a parameter controlling the deviation between the trained model <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and SFT model <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\textup{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\textup{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, since we do not want the trained model to be too far away from the SFT model. <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is a distribution of prompts, and the optimal solution to Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is independent of <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, which will be presented in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Direct Preference Optimization (DPO).</span>
Recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al.(2023)Rafailov, Sharma, Mitchell, Manning, Ermon, and
Finn</a>]</cite> designed an direct preference optimization (DPO) approach, which bypasses the reward model training phase in RLHF, and directly fine-tunes LLMs using preference data. The derivation idea of DPO is as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">First, the optimal solution to Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peters &amp; Schaal(2007)Peters and Schaal</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al.(2019)Peng, Kumar, Zhang, and Levine</a>]</cite></p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi^{*}_{r}(y|x)=\frac{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}r(x,y)\right)}{Z_{r}(x)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi^{*}_{r}(y|x)=\frac{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}r(x,y)\right)}{Z_{r}(x)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{r}(x):=\sum_{y^{\prime}\in\mathcal{Y}}\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{\prime}|x)\exp(\frac{1}{\beta}r(x,y^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{r}(x):=\sum_{y^{\prime}\in\mathcal{Y}}\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{\prime}|x)\exp(\frac{1}{\beta}r(x,y^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math> is the partition function. Then, we can rewrite Eq. (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) to express the reward function <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> by the optimal policy <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r(x,y)=\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r}(y|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)}+\beta\log Z_{r}(x)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r(x,y)=\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r}(y|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)}+\beta\log Z_{r}(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.3" class="ltx_p">Eqs. (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) hold for any reward function <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, the Bradley-Terry model in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) can be expressed by the optimal policy <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Pr\left[y^{rw}\succ y^{r\ell}|x\right]=\sigma\left(\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}(y^{rw}|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{rw}|x)}-\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}(y^{r\ell}|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{r\ell}|x)}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≻</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Pr\left[y^{rw}\succ y^{r\ell}|x\right]=\sigma\left(\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}(y^{rw}|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{rw}|x)}-\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}(y^{r\ell}|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{r\ell}|x)}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.4" class="ltx_p">where the partition function <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{r^{*}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{r^{*}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is cancelled out. Now, by expressing the probability that preference data happen by <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can replace the likelihood in the MLE training objective in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) by Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), and obtain a new objective with the optimization variable directly being the policy:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\pi}\ -\frac{1}{N^{r}}\sum_{i=1}^{N^{r}}\log\sigma\left(\beta\log\frac{\pi(y^{rw}_{i}|x^{r}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{rw}_{i}|x^{r}_{i})}-\beta\log\frac{\pi(y^{r\ell}_{i}|x^{r}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{r\ell}_{i}|x^{r}_{i})}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.500em">−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\pi}\ -\frac{1}{N^{r}}\sum_{i=1}^{N^{r}}\log\sigma\left(\beta\log\frac{\pi(y^{rw}_{i}|x^{r}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{rw}_{i}|x^{r}_{i})}-\beta\log\frac{\pi(y^{r\ell}_{i}|x^{r}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{r\ell}_{i}|x^{r}_{i})}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.5" class="ltx_p">Eq. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) is the training objective of DPO. Thus, DPO directly uses preference data to fine-tune LLMs without training a reward model, and enjoys lower memory and computational costs than RLHF.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Safe RLHF.</span> To enhance safety in LLM alignment, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>]</cite> proposed a safe RLHF framework. In safe RLHF, we assume that there exists an <em id="S3.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unknown</em> cost function <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}(x,y)\in[-C_{\max},C_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}(x,y)\in[-C_{\max},C_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math> for some constant <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>, which characterizes the harmfulness of response <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> under prompt <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. We have access to a cost preference dataset <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{c}=\{x^{c}_{i},y^{cw}_{i},y^{c\ell}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N^{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{c}=\{x^{c}_{i},y^{cw}_{i},y^{c\ell}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N^{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{cw}_{i},y^{c\ell}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{cw}_{i},y^{c\ell}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote unsafer and safer responses under prompt <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{c}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{c}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{cw}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{cw}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has a higher cost than <math id="S3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{c\ell}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{c\ell}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>), and the superscript <math id="S3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> stands for cost preference. We assume that cost preference is generated according to the Bradley-Terry model with cost function <math id="S3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Pr\left[y^{cw}\succ y^{c\ell}|x\right]=\frac{\exp(c^{*}(x,y^{cw}))}{\exp(c^{*}(x,y^{cw}))+\exp(c^{*}(x,y^{c\ell}))}=\sigma\left(c^{*}(x,y^{cw})-c^{*}(x,y^{c\ell})\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≻</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Pr\left[y^{cw}\succ y^{c\ell}|x\right]=\frac{\exp(c^{*}(x,y^{cw}))}{\exp(c^{*}(x,y^{cw}))+\exp(c^{*}(x,y^{c\ell}))}=\sigma\left(c^{*}(x,y^{cw})-c^{*}(x,y^{c\ell})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Similar to Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), we can also train a cost model <math id="S3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> via MLE:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{c}\ -\frac{1}{N^{c}}\sum_{i=1}^{N^{c}}\log\sigma\left(c(x^{c}_{i},y^{cw}_{i})-c(x^{c}_{i},y^{c\ell}_{i})\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>c</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.500em">−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{c}\ -\frac{1}{N^{c}}\sum_{i=1}^{N^{c}}\log\sigma\left(c(x^{c}_{i},y^{cw}_{i})-c(x^{c}_{i},y^{c\ell}_{i})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p7.3" class="ltx_p">To restrict the costs of LLM outputs within a threshold, we consider the constrained optimization:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\pi}\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\pi}\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}}\left[\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi(\cdot|x)}\left[r(x,y)\right]-\beta\cdot\textup{KL}\left(\pi(\cdot|x)\|\pi_{\textup{ref}}(\cdot|x)\right)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}}\left[\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi(\cdot|x)}\left[r(x,y)\right]-\beta\cdot\textup{KL}\left(\pi(\cdot|x)\|\pi_{\textup{ref}}(\cdot|x)\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.Ex2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">s.t.</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle c(x,y)\leq 0,\quad\forall x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p},y\sim\pi(\cdot|x)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle c(x,y)\leq 0,\quad\forall x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p},y\sim\pi(\cdot|x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p7.4" class="ltx_p">Here for simplicity, we set the threshold of harmfulness to <math id="S3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The above problem is hard to solve using neural networks, since it requires the cost of every possible response <math id="S3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> to stay below <math id="S3.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">To feasibly perform safety alignment, many prior works, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wachi et al.(2024)Wachi, Tran, Sato, Tanabe, and
Akimoto</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al.(2024b)Liu, Sun, and Zheng</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al.(2025)Kim, Jang, Kim, Kim, Lee, Bae, and
Lee</a>]</cite>, consider a relaxed optimization problem with an expected cost constraint, which we called <em id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">constrained alignment problem</em>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\pi}\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\pi}\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle f(\pi):=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}}\left[\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi(\cdot|x)}\left[r^{*}(x,y)\right]-\beta\cdot\textup{KL}\left(\pi(\cdot|x)\|\pi_{\textup{ref}}(\cdot|x)\right)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(\pi):=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}}\left[\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi(\cdot|x)}\left[r^{*}(x,y)\right]-\beta\cdot\textup{KL}\left(\pi(\cdot|x)\|\pi_{\textup{ref}}(\cdot|x)\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.E10.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">s.t.</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle g(\pi):=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p},y\sim\pi(\cdot|x)}[c^{*}(x,y)]\leq 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g(\pi):=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p},y\sim\pi(\cdot|x)}[c^{*}(x,y)]\leq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p8.2" class="ltx_p">In this work, we also study this relaxed problem.
Then, it is natural to look into the Lagrangian dual problem of the above constrained optimization:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\lambda\geq 0}\ \max_{\pi}\ L(\pi;\lambda):=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}}\left[\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi(\cdot|x)}\left[r^{*}(x,y)\!-\!\lambda\cdot c^{*}(x,y)\right]\!-\!\beta\!\cdot\!\textup{KL}\left(\pi(\cdot|x)\|\pi_{\textup{ref}}(\cdot|x)\right)\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">−</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">⋅</mo><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\lambda\geq 0}\ \max_{\pi}\ L(\pi;\lambda):=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}}\left[\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi(\cdot|x)}\left[r^{*}(x,y)\!-\!\lambda\cdot c^{*}(x,y)\right]\!-\!\beta\!\cdot\!\textup{KL}\left(\pi(\cdot|x)\|\pi_{\textup{ref}}(\cdot|x)\right)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p8.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> is a Lagrange multiplier. Throughout the paper, we call <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(\pi;\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(\pi;\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> the <em id="S3.p8.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Lagrangian function</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">With the above unconstrained problem, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>]</cite> regarded <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r-\lambda\cdot c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r-\lambda\cdot c</annotation></semantics></math> as a new reward function and applied an RL algorithm PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al.(2017)Schulman, Wolski, Dhariwal, Radford, and
Klimov</a>]</cite> to maximize <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(\pi;\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(\pi;\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, and performed subgradient descent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beck(2017)</a>]</cite> to update <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. This safe RLHF framework requires to train both reward and cost models, which incurs high memory and computational costs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we propose a provably efficient primal-dual DPO approach for the constrained alignment problem (Eq. (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>)), utilizing a model trained using standard DPO on reward preference data to provide reward information.
We first describe the key idea behind our approach, and present the specific algorithm <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> which has rigorous theoretical guarantees.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Our Approach</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, we have that the optimal solution to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{\pi}L(\pi;\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\pi}L(\pi;\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) is</p>
<table id="S4.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r(x,y)-\lambda\cdot c(x,y)=\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r-\lambda\cdot c}(y|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)}+\beta\log Z_{r-\lambda\cdot c}(x)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r(x,y)-\lambda\cdot c(x,y)=\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r-\lambda\cdot c}(y|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)}+\beta\log Z_{r-\lambda\cdot c}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{r-\lambda\cdot c}(x):=\sum_{y^{\prime}\in\mathcal{Y}}\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{\prime}|x)\exp(\frac{1}{\beta}\left(r(x,y^{\prime})-\lambda\cdot c(x,y^{\prime})\right))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{r-\lambda\cdot c}(x):=\sum_{y^{\prime}\in\mathcal{Y}}\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{\prime}|x)\exp(\frac{1}{\beta}\left(r(x,y^{\prime})-\lambda\cdot c(x,y^{\prime})\right))</annotation></semantics></math> is the partition function, and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> can be any reward and cost functions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When one wants to apply the derivation idea of DPO in Eqs. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), a difficulty arises: <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">We do not have preference data generated according to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r-\lambda\cdot c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r-\lambda\cdot c</annotation></semantics></math></em>, but only have preference data generated according to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> separately. Thus, we cannot use <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r-\lambda\cdot c}(y|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r-\lambda\cdot c}(y|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)}</annotation></semantics></math> to directly express data likelihood as in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), which means that the DPO derivation idea cannot be directly applied here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To overcome this difficulty,
we first rearrange Eq. (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In 4.1 Our Approach ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle c(x,y)=\frac{1}{\lambda}\left(r(x,y)-\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r-\lambda\cdot c}(y|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)}-\beta\log Z_{r-\lambda\cdot c}(x)\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle c(x,y)=\frac{1}{\lambda}\left(r(x,y)-\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r-\lambda\cdot c}(y|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)}-\beta\log Z_{r-\lambda\cdot c}(x)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Plugging the above equation with <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> into Eq. (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), the generation of cost preference data can be rewritten as <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr[y^{cw}\succ y^{c\ell}|x]=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≻</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr[y^{cw}\succ y^{c\ell}|x]=</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S4.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma\Bigg(\frac{1}{\lambda}\bigg(r^{*}(x,y^{cw})-\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r^{*}-\lambda\cdot c^{*}}(y^{cw}|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{cw}|x)}-\Big(r^{*}(x,y^{c\ell})-\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r^{*}-\lambda\cdot c^{*}}(y^{c\ell}|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{c\ell}|x)}\Big)\bigg)\Bigg)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma\Bigg(\frac{1}{\lambda}\bigg(r^{*}(x,y^{cw})-\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r^{*}-\lambda\cdot c^{*}}(y^{cw}|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{cw}|x)}-\Big(r^{*}(x,y^{c\ell})-\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r^{*}-\lambda\cdot c^{*}}(y^{c\ell}|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{c\ell}|x)}\Big)\bigg)\Bigg),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{r^{*}-\lambda\cdot c^{*}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{r^{*}-\lambda\cdot c^{*}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is cancelled out.
Then, replacing the cost preference data likelihood in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) by the above equation, we can obtain a training objective with the optimization variable directly being the policy which is supposed to get close to <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}_{r^{*}-\lambda\cdot c^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}_{r^{*}-\lambda\cdot c^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> during training:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{c}\ -\frac{1}{N^{c}}\sum_{i=1}^{N^{c}}\log\sigma\left(\frac{1}{\lambda}\left(r^{*}(x^{c}_{i},y^{cw}_{i})-\beta L_{\pi}(y^{cw}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})-\left(r^{*}(x^{c}_{i},y^{c\ell}_{i})-\beta L_{\pi}(y^{c\ell}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})\right)\right)\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>c</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.500em">−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{c}\ -\frac{1}{N^{c}}\sum_{i=1}^{N^{c}}\log\sigma\left(\frac{1}{\lambda}\left(r^{*}(x^{c}_{i},y^{cw}_{i})-\beta L_{\pi}(y^{cw}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})-\left(r^{*}(x^{c}_{i},y^{c\ell}_{i})-\beta L_{\pi}(y^{c\ell}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})\right)\right)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\pi}(y|x):=\log\frac{\pi(y|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\pi}(y|x):=\log\frac{\pi(y|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the logarithmic ratio of response <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> between <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\textup{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\textup{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Now the main challenge lies in that <em id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">we do not know <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></em>, and meanwhile, we do not want to explicitly train a reward model in order to keep memory and computational efficiency. To handle this challenge, we make an observation that <em id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}(x^{c}_{i},y^{cw}_{i})-r^{*}(x^{c}_{i},y^{c\ell}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}(x^{c}_{i},y^{cw}_{i})-r^{*}(x^{c}_{i},y^{c\ell}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed by <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}(y^{cw}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{cw}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})}-\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}(y^{c\ell}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{c\ell}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}(y^{cw}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{cw}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})}-\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}(y^{c\ell}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{c\ell}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math></em> according to Eq. (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
Then, <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> is what we can learn by training a model using standard DPO on reward preference data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, using this observation, the training objective Eq. (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In 4.1 Our Approach ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) can be rewritten as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{c}\ -\frac{1}{N^{c}}\sum_{i=1}^{N^{c}}\log\sigma\left(\frac{\beta}{\lambda}\left(L_{\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}}(y^{cw}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})\!-\!L_{\pi}(y^{cw}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})-\left(L_{\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}}(y^{c\ell}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})\!-\!L_{\pi}(y^{c\ell}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})\right)\right)\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>c</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.500em">−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.052em">−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{c}\ -\frac{1}{N^{c}}\sum_{i=1}^{N^{c}}\log\sigma\left(\frac{\beta}{\lambda}\left(L_{\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}}(y^{cw}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})\!-\!L_{\pi}(y^{cw}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})-\left(L_{\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}}(y^{c\ell}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})\!-\!L_{\pi}(y^{c\ell}_{i}|x^{c}_{i})\right)\right)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be learned by first training a model using standard DPO on reward preference data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Eq. (<a href="#S4.E14" title="In 4.1 Our Approach ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) is the main idea of our primal-dual DPO approach utilizing standard DPO. This approach avoids training reward and cost models, which significantly reduces memory and computational costs compared to prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al.(2024b)Liu, Sun, and Zheng</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al.(2024)Huang, Li, Dobriban, Bastani, Hassani, and
Ding</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al.(2025)Zhang, Li, Hounie, Bastani, Ding, and
Ribeiro</a>]</cite>. Our approach shows even more advantages when there already exists a trained model on reward preference data, which is often the case since there are many high-quality and open-source LLMs, e.g., LLaMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al.(2024)Dubey, Jauhri, Pandey, Kadian, Al-Dahle, Letman,
Mathur, Schelten, Yang, Fan, et al.</a>]</cite> and Gemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al.(2024a)Team, Mesnard, Hardin, Dadashi,
Bhupatiraju, Pathak, Sifre, Rivière, Kale, Love, et al.</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al.(2024b)Team, Riviere, Pathak, Sessa, Hardin,
Bhupatiraju, Hussenot, Mesnard, Shahriari, Ramé, et al.</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al.(2025)Team, Kamath, Ferret, Pathak, Vieillard, Merhej,
Perrin, Matejovicova, Ramé, Rivière, et al.</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>A Provably Efficient Algorithm <math id="S4.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While Eq. (<a href="#S4.E14" title="In 4.1 Our Approach ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) has presented the main idea of our primal-dual DPO approach, to enable rigorous theoretical guarantees, we develop a specific provably efficient algorithm <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math>, which imposes policy search constraints based on Eq. (<a href="#S4.E14" title="In 4.1 Our Approach ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) and enjoys suboptimality and constraint violation guarantees.
Before describing the specific algorithm <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math>, we first introduce several assumptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Slater’s Condition)<span id="Thmassumption1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a policy <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> which satisfies <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p},y\sim\bar{\pi}(\cdot|x)}[c^{*}(x,y)]&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p},y\sim\bar{\pi}(\cdot|x)}[c^{*}(x,y)]&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
In addition, we know a constant <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\geq\frac{f(\pi^{*})-f(\bar{\pi})}{-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p},y\sim\bar{\pi}(\cdot|x)}[c^{*}(x,y)]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\geq\frac{f(\pi^{*})-f(\bar{\pi})}{-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p},y\sim\bar{\pi}(\cdot|x)}[c^{*}(x,y)]}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This assumption is common in the constrained optimization and learning literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beck(2017)</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Efroni et al.(2020)Efroni, Mannor, and Pirotta</a>]</cite>. In practice, it is reasonable that there exists a safe policy model, e.g., a language model which refuses to answer harmful questions albeit less helpful.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Following prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al.(2025)Kim, Jang, Kim, Kim, Lee, Bae, and
Lee</a>]</cite>, we also allow querying cost binary feedback from human annotators, which indicates whether a response <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is safe under prompt <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Such cost binary feedback is generated according to</p>
<table id="S4.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Pr\left[Z(y)=1|x\right]=\sigma\left(c^{*}(x,y)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Pr\left[Z(y)=1|x\right]=\sigma\left(c^{*}(x,y)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr[Z(y)=0|x]=1-\sigma\left(c^{*}(x,y)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr[Z(y)=0|x]=1-\sigma\left(c^{*}(x,y)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(y)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(y)=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(y)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(y)=0</annotation></semantics></math> denote that <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is unsafe and safe, respectively.
We will use cost binary feedback in algorithm <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> to estimate the cost of the current trained model for Lagrange multiplier update.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span><math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> </span>​<math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta,\!\pi_{\textup{ref}},\rho,\lambda_{1},K,N^{\textup{CE}},M^{\textup{CE}},\mathcal{D}^{p},\mathcal{D}^{r}\!=\!\{(x^{r}_{i},y_{i}^{rw},y_{i}^{r\ell})\}_{\!i\in[N^{r}]},\!\mathcal{D}^{c}\!=\!\{(x^{c}_{i},y_{i}^{cw},y_{i}^{c\ell})\}_{\!i\in[N^{c}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.108em">=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.108em">=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta,\!\pi_{\textup{ref}},\rho,\lambda_{1},K,N^{\textup{CE}},M^{\textup{CE}},\mathcal{D}^{p},\mathcal{D}^{r}\!=\!\{(x^{r}_{i},y_{i}^{rw},y_{i}^{r\ell})\}_{\!i\in[N^{r}]},\!\mathcal{D}^{c}\!=\!\{(x^{c}_{i},y_{i}^{cw},y_{i}^{c\ell})\}_{\!i\in[N^{c}]}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
            Train a model using standard DPO on reward preference data:

<table id="S4.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi^{*}_{\hat{r}}\leftarrow\operatornamewithlimits{argmin}_{\pi\in\Pi^{r}}-\frac{1}{N^{r}}\sum_{i=1}^{N^{r}}\log\sigma\left(\beta\log\frac{\pi(y_{i}^{rw}|x^{r}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y_{i}^{rw}|x^{r}_{i})}-\beta\log\frac{\pi(y_{i}^{r\ell}|x^{r}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y_{i}^{r\ell}|x^{r}_{i})}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">argmin</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi^{*}_{\hat{r}}\leftarrow\operatornamewithlimits{argmin}_{\pi\in\Pi^{r}}-\frac{1}{N^{r}}\sum_{i=1}^{N^{r}}\log\sigma\left(\beta\log\frac{\pi(y_{i}^{rw}|x^{r}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y_{i}^{rw}|x^{r}_{i})}-\beta\log\frac{\pi(y_{i}^{r\ell}|x^{r}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y_{i}^{r\ell}|x^{r}_{i})}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
where <math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E17" title='In 4.2 A Provably Efficient Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>)

</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.3.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,2,\dots,K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,2,\dots,K</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
            Train a model using a rearranged Lagrangian DPO objective on cost preference data:

<table id="S4.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{k}\leftarrow\operatornamewithlimits{argmin}_{\pi\in\Pi^{c}_{k}}-\frac{1}{N^{c}}\sum_{i=1}^{N^{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">argmin</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{k}\leftarrow\operatornamewithlimits{argmin}_{\pi\in\Pi^{c}_{k}}-\frac{1}{N^{c}}\sum_{i=1}^{N^{c}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\log\sigma\Bigg(\frac{1}{\lambda_{k}}\bigg(\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{\hat{r}}(y_{i}^{cw}|x^{c}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y_{i}^{cw}|x^{c}_{i})}-\beta\log\frac{\pi(y_{i}^{cw}|x^{c}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y_{i}^{cw}|x^{c}_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\log\sigma\Bigg(\frac{1}{\lambda_{k}}\bigg(\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{\hat{r}}(y_{i}^{cw}|x^{c}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y_{i}^{cw}|x^{c}_{i})}-\beta\log\frac{\pi(y_{i}^{cw}|x^{c}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y_{i}^{cw}|x^{c}_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Big(\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{\hat{r}}(y_{i}^{c\ell}|x^{c}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y_{i}^{c\ell}|x^{c}_{i})}-\beta\log\frac{\pi(y_{i}^{c\ell}|x^{c}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y_{i}^{c\ell}|x^{c}_{i})}\Big)\bigg)\Bigg)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Big(\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{\hat{r}}(y_{i}^{c\ell}|x^{c}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y_{i}^{c\ell}|x^{c}_{i})}-\beta\log\frac{\pi(y_{i}^{c\ell}|x^{c}_{i})}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y_{i}^{c\ell}|x^{c}_{i})}\Big)\bigg)\Bigg),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
where <math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{c}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{c}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E18" title='In 4.2 A Provably Efficient Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>)

</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
            Construct an estimate <math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{c}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{c}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p},y\sim\pi_{k}(\cdot|x)}[c^{*}(x,y)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p},y\sim\pi_{k}(\cdot|x)}[c^{*}(x,y)]</annotation></semantics></math>: For <math id="algorithm1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,N^{\textup{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,N^{\textup{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math>, first sample <math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y_{i}\sim\pi_{k}(\cdot|x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}\sim\pi_{k}(\cdot|x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for each <math id="algorithm1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i},y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i},y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, sample <math id="algorithm1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{Z_{i,j}\}_{j=1}^{M^{\textup{CE}}}\overset{\textup{i.i.d.}}{\sim}\textup{Ber}(\sigma(c^{*}(x_{i},y_{i})))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mtext mathsize="0.700em">i.i.d.</mtext></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>Ber</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{Z_{i,j}\}_{j=1}^{M^{\textup{CE}}}\overset{\textup{i.i.d.}}{\sim}\textup{Ber}(\sigma(c^{*}(x_{i},y_{i})))</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="algorithm1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{c}_{k}\leftarrow\frac{1}{N^{\textup{CE}}}\sum_{i=1}^{N^{\textup{CE}}}\sigma^{-1}(\frac{1}{M^{\textup{CE}}}\sum_{j=1}^{M^{\textup{CE}}}Z_{i,j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>N</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>M</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup></msubsup><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{c}_{k}\leftarrow\frac{1}{N^{\textup{CE}}}\sum_{i=1}^{N^{\textup{CE}}}\sigma^{-1}(\frac{1}{M^{\textup{CE}}}\sum_{j=1}^{M^{\textup{CE}}}Z_{i,j})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="algorithm1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{-1}(z):=\log(\frac{1}{1-z}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{-1}(z):=\log(\frac{1}{1-z}-1)</annotation></semantics></math> is the inverse of the sigmoid function 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k+1}\leftarrow\textup{Proj}_{[0,2\rho]}(\lambda_{k}+\eta\tilde{c}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Proj</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k+1}\leftarrow\textup{Proj}_{[0,2\rho]}(\lambda_{k}+\eta\tilde{c}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="algorithm1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta:=\frac{\lambda_{1}}{C_{\max}\sqrt{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>K</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta:=\frac{\lambda_{1}}{C_{\max}\sqrt{K}}</annotation></semantics></math> 

</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="algorithm1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\textup{out}}_{K}:=\textup{unif}(\pi_{1},\dots,\pi_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>K</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mtext>unif</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\textup{out}}_{K}:=\textup{unif}(\pi_{1},\dots,\pi_{K})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Now we present an efficient and provably convergent primal-dual DPO algorithm <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> first trains a model using standard DPO on reward preference data, and leverages this model to provide reward information to train a model using a rearranged Lagrangian DPO objective with policy search constraints. Then, <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> conducts projected subgradient descent to update the Lagrange multiplier. <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> performs such model training and Lagrange multiplier update alternately.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title='In 4.2 A Provably Efficient Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates the algorithm procedure of <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> first trains a model <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}_{\hat{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}_{\hat{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> using the standard DPO objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al.(2023)Rafailov, Sharma, Mitchell, Manning, Ermon, and
Finn</a>]</cite> on reward preference data <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> within a constrained policy search range:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Pi^{r}:=\left\{\pi(y|x)=\frac{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)\cdot\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}r(x,y)\right)}{\sum_{y^{\prime}\in\mathcal{Y}}\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{\prime}|x)\cdot\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}r(x,y^{\prime})\right)}:r\in[-R_{\max},R_{\max}]\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Pi^{r}:=\left\{\pi(y|x)=\frac{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)\cdot\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}r(x,y)\right)}{\sum_{y^{\prime}\in\mathcal{Y}}\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{\prime}|x)\cdot\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}r(x,y^{\prime})\right)}:r\in[-R_{\max},R_{\max}]\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Since we use only finite preference data, we cannot exactly learn <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}_{r^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Instead, we learn a reward function <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{r}</annotation></semantics></math> which is close to <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and implicitly maintained by the policy <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}_{\hat{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}_{\hat{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The notation <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}_{\hat{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}_{\hat{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the optimal policy under the learned reward function <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{r}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>)).
The policy search range <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is used to restrict the learned reward function <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{r}</annotation></semantics></math> within <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-R_{\max},R_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-R_{\max},R_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math> (Line <a href="#S4.EGx19" title='In 1 ‣ 4.2 A Provably Efficient Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Next,
in each iteration <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, given a Lagrange multiplier <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> utilizes the reward information provided by <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}_{\hat{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}_{\hat{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> to train a model using a rearranged Lagrangian DPO objective as derived in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Our Approach ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, but with a constrained policy search range:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Pi^{c}_{k}\!:=\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">:=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Pi^{c}_{k}\!:=\!</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\{\!\pi(y|x)\!=\!\frac{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}\left(\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{\hat{r}}(y|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)}-\lambda_{k}c(x,y)\right)\right)}{\sum_{y^{\prime}\in\mathcal{Y}}\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{\prime}|x)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}\left(\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{\hat{r}}(y^{\prime}|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{\prime}|x)}-\lambda_{k}c(x,y^{\prime})\right)\right)}\!\!:c\!\in\![-C_{\max},C_{\max}]\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\{\!\pi(y|x)\!=\!\frac{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}\left(\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{\hat{r}}(y|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)}-\lambda_{k}c(x,y)\right)\right)}{\sum_{y^{\prime}\in\mathcal{Y}}\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{\prime}|x)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}\left(\beta\log\frac{\pi^{*}_{\hat{r}}(y^{\prime}|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{\prime}|x)}-\lambda_{k}c(x,y^{\prime})\right)\right)}\!\!:c\!\in\![-C_{\max},C_{\max}]\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overset{\textup{(a)}}{=}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mtext mathsize="0.700em">(a)</mtext></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overset{\textup{(a)}}{=}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\{\pi(y|x)\!=\!\frac{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}\left(\hat{r}(x,y)-\lambda_{k}c(x,y)\right)\right)}{\sum_{y^{\prime}\in\mathcal{Y}}\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{\prime}|x)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}\left(\hat{r}(x,y^{\prime})-\lambda_{k}c(x,y^{\prime})\right)\right)}\!:c\!\in\![-C_{\max},C_{\max}]\right\}\!." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.330em;" width="0.330em"><mo>}</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\{\pi(y|x)\!=\!\frac{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y|x)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}\left(\hat{r}(x,y)-\lambda_{k}c(x,y)\right)\right)}{\sum_{y^{\prime}\in\mathcal{Y}}\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{\prime}|x)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}\left(\hat{r}(x,y^{\prime})-\lambda_{k}c(x,y^{\prime})\right)\right)}\!:c\!\in\![-C_{\max},C_{\max}]\right\}\!.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Here equality (a) is due to Eq. (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) and the fact that the partition function <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\hat{r}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\hat{r}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> only depends on <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and can be cancelled out in the softmax form. <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{c}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{c}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is used to restrict the learned cost function within <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-C_{\max},C_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-C_{\max},C_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math> (Line <a href="#S4.EGx20" title='In 1 ‣ 4.2 A Provably Efficient Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">After obtaining <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, we estimate the cost of <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for Lagrange multiplier update using the following scheme: We i.i.d. draw <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\textup{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\textup{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> prompt-response pairs <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(x_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N^{\textup{CE}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(x_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N^{\textup{CE}}}</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each pair <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i},y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i},y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, we i.i.d. query human annotators whether response <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is safe under prompt <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\textup{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\textup{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> times, and obtain <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\textup{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\textup{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> cost binary feedback <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{Z_{i,j}\}_{j=1}^{M^{\textup{CE}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{Z_{i,j}\}_{j=1}^{M^{\textup{CE}}}</annotation></semantics></math> drawn from <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{Ber}(\sigma(c^{*}(x_{i},y_{i})))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Ber</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{Ber}(\sigma(c^{*}(x_{i},y_{i})))</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we take the inverse of the sigmoid function <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{-1}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{-1}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> on the average of these <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\textup{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\textup{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> Bernoulli outcomes to obtain an estimate <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{c}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{c}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for the expected cost of <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> (Line <a href="#algorithm1" title='In 4.2 A Provably Efficient Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). In analysis, we can bound the deviation between this estimate <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{c}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{c}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and the expected cost of <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title='C.2 Cost Estimation for Lagrangian Multiplier Update ‣ Appendix C Proofs for Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>).
After cost estimation, <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> performs projected subgradient descent with <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{c}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{c}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to update Lagrange multiplier <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and enters the next iteration (Line <a href="#algorithm1" title='In 4.2 A Provably Efficient Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Theoretical Guarantees of Algorithm <math id="S4.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al.(2024b)Liu, Sun, and Zheng</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al.(2025)Kim, Jang, Kim, Kim, Lee, Bae, and
Lee</a>]</cite> which do not provide theoretical guarantees for their output policy models, we establish rigid suboptimality and constraint violation guarantees for the output policy of algorithm <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, we note that our rearranged Lagrangian DPO objective (Eq. (<a href="#S4.E16" title='In 3 ‣ 1 ‣ 4.2 A Provably Efficient Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>)) and the safe RLHF procedure, which first trains reward and cost models using MLE and maximizes <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(\pi;\lambda_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(\pi;\lambda_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> under the learned reward and cost functions, have the same set of optimal solutions (see Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title='Theorem 4 (Connection between Our Rearranged Lagrangian DPO and Safe RLHF). ‣ Step (ii). ‣ C.1 Connection between Our DPO-based Procedure and the RLHF-based Procedure ‣ Appendix C Proofs for Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title='C.1 Connection between Our DPO-based Procedure and the RLHF-based Procedure ‣ Appendix C Proofs for Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a> for a formal statement). Next, we present the theoretical results of algorithm <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\in\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\in\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> denote a <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{X}||\mathcal{Y}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{X}||\mathcal{Y}|</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector where the entry corresponding to <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and all other entries are <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(z):=\sqrt{\left(\exp\left(z\right)+\exp\left(-z\right)+2\right)^{2}\left(|\mathcal{X}||\mathcal{Y}|+\log\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)\right)+\gamma z^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(z):=\sqrt{\left(\exp\left(z\right)+\exp\left(-z\right)+2\right)^{2}\left(|\mathcal{X}||\mathcal{Y}|+\log\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)\right)+\gamma z^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S4.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B:=\rho C_{\max}\sqrt{\frac{\log\left(\frac{K}{\delta}\right)}{N^{\textup{CE}}}}+\rho W\sqrt{\frac{\log\left(\frac{|\mathcal{X}||\mathcal{Y}|N^{\textup{CE}}K}{\delta}\right)}{M^{\textup{CE}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>K</mi><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B:=\rho C_{\max}\sqrt{\frac{\log\left(\frac{K}{\delta}\right)}{N^{\textup{CE}}}}+\rho W\sqrt{\frac{\log\left(\frac{|\mathcal{X}||\mathcal{Y}|N^{\textup{CE}}K}{\delta}\right)}{M^{\textup{CE}}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle+\!\rho\!\cdot\!\alpha(C_{\max})\!\bigg(\!\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}\times\pi^{*}}\!\!\left[\!\left\|\phi(x,y)\right\|_{(\Sigma_{\mathcal{D}^{c}}+\gamma I)^{-1}}\!\right]\!+\!\frac{1}{K}\!\sum_{k=1}^{K}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}\times\pi_{k}}\!\!\left[\!\left\|\phi(x,y)\right\|_{(\Sigma_{\mathcal{D}^{c}}+\gamma I)^{-1}}\!\right]\!\bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.052em">+</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">⋅</mo><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mpadded></mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo>]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\!\rho\!\cdot\!\alpha(C_{\max})\!\bigg(\!\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}\times\pi^{*}}\!\!\left[\!\left\|\phi(x,y)\right\|_{(\Sigma_{\mathcal{D}^{c}}+\gamma I)^{-1}}\!\right]\!+\!\frac{1}{K}\!\sum_{k=1}^{K}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}\times\pi_{k}}\!\!\left[\!\left\|\phi(x,y)\right\|_{(\Sigma_{\mathcal{D}^{c}}+\gamma I)^{-1}}\!\right]\!\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\!\alpha(R_{\max})\!\bigg(\!\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}\times\pi^{*}}\!\left[\!\left\|\phi(x,y)\right\|_{(\Sigma_{\mathcal{D}^{r}}+\gamma I)^{-1}}\!\right]\!+\!\frac{1}{K}\!\sum_{k=1}^{K}\!\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}\times\pi_{k}}\!\left[\!\left\|\phi(x,y)\right\|_{(\Sigma_{\mathcal{D}^{r}}+\gamma I)^{-1}}\!\right]\!\bigg)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo>]</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\!\alpha(R_{\max})\!\bigg(\!\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}\times\pi^{*}}\!\left[\!\left\|\phi(x,y)\right\|_{(\Sigma_{\mathcal{D}^{r}}+\gamma I)^{-1}}\!\right]\!+\!\frac{1}{K}\!\sum_{k=1}^{K}\!\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}\times\pi_{k}}\!\left[\!\left\|\phi(x,y)\right\|_{(\Sigma_{\mathcal{D}^{r}}+\gamma I)^{-1}}\!\right]\!\bigg).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\mathcal{D}^{\diamond}}:=\sum_{(x,y,y^{\prime})\in\mathcal{D}^{\diamond}}\left(\phi(x,y)-\phi(x,y^{\prime})\right)\left(\phi(x,y)-\phi(x,y^{\prime}\right)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⋄</mo></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⋄</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\mathcal{D}^{\diamond}}:=\sum_{(x,y,y^{\prime})\in\mathcal{D}^{\diamond}}\left(\phi(x,y)-\phi(x,y^{\prime})\right)\left(\phi(x,y)-\phi(x,y^{\prime}\right)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\diamond\in\{r,c\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⋄</mo><mo lspace="0em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diamond\in\{r,c\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}\times\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}\times\pi</annotation></semantics></math> denotes <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\sim\mathcal{D}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y\sim\pi(\cdot|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\sim\pi(\cdot|x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is an arbitrary regularization parameter. <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is a parameter dependent on <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is formally defined in Eq. (<a href="#A3.E28" title='In Lemma 2. ‣ C.2 Cost Estimation for Lagrangian Multiplier Update ‣ Appendix C Proofs for Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>) in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title='C.2 Cost Estimation for Lagrangian Multiplier Update ‣ Appendix C Proofs for Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Result of Algorithm <math id="Thmtheorem1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With probability at least <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the output policy <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\textup{out}}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>K</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\textup{out}}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> of algorithm <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(\pi^{*})-f(\pi^{\textup{out}}_{K})=O\bigg(\frac{\lambda_{1}C_{\max}}{\sqrt{K}}+B\bigg),\quad g(\pi^{\textup{out}}_{K})=O\bigg(\frac{C_{\max}}{\rho\sqrt{K}}\Big(\frac{\left(\lambda_{1}-2\rho\right)^{2}}{\lambda_{1}}+\lambda_{1}\Big)+\frac{B}{\rho}\bigg)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>K</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><msqrt><mi>K</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>K</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>K</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>B</mi><mi>ρ</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(\pi^{*})-f(\pi^{\textup{out}}_{K})=O\bigg(\frac{\lambda_{1}C_{\max}}{\sqrt{K}}+B\bigg),\quad g(\pi^{\textup{out}}_{K})=O\bigg(\frac{C_{\max}}{\rho\sqrt{K}}\Big(\frac{\left(\lambda_{1}-2\rho\right)^{2}}{\lambda_{1}}+\lambda_{1}\Big)+\frac{B}{\rho}\bigg).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this result, the <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{\sqrt{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>K</mi></msqrt></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{\sqrt{K}}</annotation></semantics></math> term is an inherent error of the primal-dual method. The <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{\sqrt{N^{\textup{CE}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msup><mi>N</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup></msqrt></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{\sqrt{N^{\textup{CE}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{\sqrt{M^{\textup{CE}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msup><mi>M</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup></msqrt></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{\sqrt{M^{\textup{CE}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> terms are the error due to cost estimation. The four <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\phi(x,y)\|_{(\Sigma_{\mathcal{D}^{\diamond}}+\gamma I)^{-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⋄</mo></msup></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\phi(x,y)\|_{(\Sigma_{\mathcal{D}^{\diamond}}+\gamma I)^{-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> terms are the error due to inferring reward and cost information from preference data. The <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\phi(x,y)\|_{(\Sigma_{\mathcal{D}^{(\diamond)}}+\gamma I)^{-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋄</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\phi(x,y)\|_{(\Sigma_{\mathcal{D}^{(\diamond)}}+\gamma I)^{-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> factor stands for how broadly the given preference data cover.
Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title='Theorem 1 (Result of Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾). ‣ 4.3 Theoretical Guarantees of Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that the suboptimality and cost violation of the output policy by algorithm <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> can be arbitrarily close to zero, when the given preference data have sufficient coverage, and the number of preference data, the number of iterations <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, and the number of samples for cost estimation <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\textup{CE}},M^{\textup{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\textup{CE}},M^{\textup{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> are large enough.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Exploratory Primal-Dual DPO with Exploration Bonuses</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The result of algorithm <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title='Theorem 1 (Result of Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾). ‣ 4.3 Theoretical Guarantees of Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) depends on the coverage of preference data, i.e., <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\phi(x,y)\right\|_{(\Sigma_{\mathcal{D}^{\diamond}}+\gamma I)^{-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⋄</mo></msup></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\phi(x,y)\right\|_{(\Sigma_{\mathcal{D}^{\diamond}}+\gamma I)^{-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If the given preference data do not have sufficient coverage, the suboptimality and constraint violation of <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> can be unbounded.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To resolve this coverage issue, we further investigate an online setting where collecting preference data online is allowed. In this setting, we develop an exploratory primal-dual DPO algorithm <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{O\mbox{-}PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{O\mbox{-}PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math>, which incorporates exploration bonuses <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{c}_{k}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{c}_{k}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{r}_{k}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{r}_{k}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> in the rearranged Lagrangian DPO and standard DPO objectives. In algorithm <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{O\mbox{-}PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{O\mbox{-}PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math>, the trained policy has an incentive to explore uncovered prompt-response space, and
gradually expands the used preference data. We defer the pseudo-code and detailed description of <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{O\mbox{-}PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{O\mbox{-}PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> to Appendix <a href="#A4" title='Appendix D Pseudo-code and Detailed Description of Algorithm 𝙾⁢"-"⁢𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> due to space limits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We take the incorporation of <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{r}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{r}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in standard DPO as an example to explain the <em id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">intuition behind why including exploration bonuses can encourage exploration</em>. For the standard DPO objective (Eq. (<a href="#S4.E15" title='In 1 ‣ 1 ‣ 4.2 A Provably Efficient Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>)), algorithm <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{O\mbox{-}PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{O\mbox{-}PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> will subtract a <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{r}(x,y^{rw})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{r}(x,y^{rw})</annotation></semantics></math> term from the original <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\log\frac{\pi(y^{rw}|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{rw}|x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\log\frac{\pi(y^{rw}|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{rw}|x)}</annotation></semantics></math> term.
When preference data do not cover <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y^{rw})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y^{rw})</annotation></semantics></math> well, <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{r}(x,y^{rw})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{r}(x,y^{rw})</annotation></semantics></math> will be large. Then, subtracting a large value from <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\log\frac{\pi(y^{rw}|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{rw}|x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\log\frac{\pi(y^{rw}|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{rw}|x)}</annotation></semantics></math> encourages <math id="S5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> to put a higher probability on <math id="S5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{rw}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{rw}</annotation></semantics></math> to maintain the original value of <math id="S5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\log\frac{\pi(y^{rw}|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{rw}|x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\log\frac{\pi(y^{rw}|x)}{\pi_{\textup{ref}}(y^{rw}|x)}</annotation></semantics></math> which achieves the optimal value of the MLE objective function.
Thus, by incorporating exploration bonuses in the training objective, the trained policy is incentivized to explore uncovered prompt-response space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">While there have been several recent works studying DPO with exploration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al.(2024a)Liu, Lu, Zhang, Liu, Guo, Yang,
Blanchet, and Wang</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al.(2025)Xie, Foster, Krishnamurthy, Rosset, Awadallah, and
Rakhlin</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cen et al.(2025)Cen, Mei, Goshvadi, Dai, Yang, Yang, Schuurmans, Chi,
and Dai</a>]</cite>, their ideas cannot be directly applied to our work, since their approaches perform optimistic exploration mainly for the optimal policy which maximizes a certain reward function, but cannot deal with the optimism for the optimal policy of a constrained optimization. To handle the exploration for the constrained alignment problem, we adopt exploration bonuses in our rearranged Lagrangian DPO training objective, and develop new analysis to get rid of offline dataset dependence. This design of DPO with bonuses and its analysis are novel to the RLHF literature.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Now we provide the suboptimality and constraint violation guarantees of algorithm <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{O\mbox{-}PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{O\mbox{-}PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(z):=\sqrt{(\exp\left(z\right)+\exp\left(-z\right)+2)^{2}\cdot(|\mathcal{X}||\mathcal{Y}|+\log(\frac{K}{\delta}))/N^{\textup{on}}+\gamma^{\textup{on}}z^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mi>K</mi><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(z):=\sqrt{(\exp\left(z\right)+\exp\left(-z\right)+2)^{2}\cdot(|\mathcal{X}||\mathcal{Y}|+\log(\frac{K}{\delta}))/N^{\textup{on}}+\gamma^{\textup{on}}z^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S5.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle B^{\textup{on}}:=\rho C_{\max}\sqrt{\frac{\log\left(\frac{K}{\delta}\right)}{N^{\textup{CE}}}}+\rho W\sqrt{\frac{\log\left(\frac{|\mathcal{X}||\mathcal{Y}|N^{\textup{CE}}K}{\delta}\right)}{M^{\textup{CE}}}}+\left(\rho\cdot\omega(C_{\max})+\omega(R_{\max})\right)\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msup><mo>:=</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>K</mi><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⋅</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B^{\textup{on}}:=\rho C_{\max}\sqrt{\frac{\log\left(\frac{K}{\delta}\right)}{N^{\textup{CE}}}}+\rho W\sqrt{\frac{\log\left(\frac{|\mathcal{X}||\mathcal{Y}|N^{\textup{CE}}K}{\delta}\right)}{M^{\textup{CE}}}}+\left(\rho\cdot\omega(C_{\max})+\omega(R_{\max})\right)\cdot</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sqrt{|\mathcal{X}||\mathcal{Y}|K\left(\log\left(\frac{\gamma^{\textup{on}}+|\mathcal{D}^{r}_{1}|+K}{|\mathcal{X}||\mathcal{Y}|\gamma^{\textup{on}}}\right)+\frac{1}{C^{\textup{base}}}\log\left(\frac{\gamma^{\textup{on}}+|\mathcal{D}^{r}_{1}|+C^{\textup{base}}K}{|\mathcal{X}||\mathcal{Y}|\gamma^{\textup{on}}}\right)\right)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>C</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sqrt{|\mathcal{X}||\mathcal{Y}|K\left(\log\left(\frac{\gamma^{\textup{on}}+|\mathcal{D}^{r}_{1}|+K}{|\mathcal{X}||\mathcal{Y}|\gamma^{\textup{on}}}\right)+\frac{1}{C^{\textup{base}}}\log\left(\frac{\gamma^{\textup{on}}+|\mathcal{D}^{r}_{1}|+C^{\textup{base}}K}{|\mathcal{X}||\mathcal{Y}|\gamma^{\textup{on}}}\right)\right)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\textup{on}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\textup{on}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of preference data collected online in each iteration. <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{\textup{on}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{\textup{on}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a given regularization parameter. <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\textup{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\textup{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a parameter related to a baseline policy which is used in online data collection. The definitions of the baseline policy and <math id="S5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\textup{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\textup{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> are in Eq. (<a href="#A4.E34" title='In Assumption 2 (Baseline Policy). ‣ Appendix D Pseudo-code and Detailed Description of Algorithm 𝙾⁢"-"⁢𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>) in Appendix <a href="#A4" title='Appendix D Pseudo-code and Detailed Description of Algorithm 𝙾⁢"-"⁢𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Result of Algorithm <math id="Thmtheorem2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{O\mbox{-}PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{O\mbox{-}PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With probability at least <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the output policy <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\textup{out}}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>K</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\textup{out}}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> of algorithm <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{O\mbox{-}PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{O\mbox{-}PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(\pi^{*})-f(\pi^{\textup{out}}_{K})=O\bigg(\frac{\lambda_{1}C_{\max}}{\sqrt{K}}+B^{\textup{on}}\bigg),\quad g(\pi^{\textup{out}}_{K})=O\bigg(\frac{C_{\max}}{\rho\sqrt{K}}\Big(\frac{\left(\lambda_{1}-2\rho\right)^{2}}{\lambda_{1}}+\lambda_{1}\Big)+\frac{B^{\textup{on}}}{\rho}\bigg)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>K</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><msqrt><mi>K</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>K</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>K</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>B</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msup><mi>ρ</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(\pi^{*})-f(\pi^{\textup{out}}_{K})=O\bigg(\frac{\lambda_{1}C_{\max}}{\sqrt{K}}+B^{\textup{on}}\bigg),\quad g(\pi^{\textup{out}}_{K})=O\bigg(\frac{C_{\max}}{\rho\sqrt{K}}\Big(\frac{\left(\lambda_{1}-2\rho\right)^{2}}{\lambda_{1}}+\lambda_{1}\Big)+\frac{B^{\textup{on}}}{\rho}\bigg).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title='Theorem 1 (Result of Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾). ‣ 4.3 Theoretical Guarantees of Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, here the results have no dependence on the coverage of preference data, i.e., <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\phi(x,y)\right\|_{(\Sigma_{\mathcal{D}^{\diamond}}+\gamma I)^{-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⋄</mo></msup></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\phi(x,y)\right\|_{(\Sigma_{\mathcal{D}^{\diamond}}+\gamma I)^{-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title='Theorem 2 (Result of Algorithm 𝙾⁢"-"⁢𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾). ‣ 5 Exploratory Primal-Dual DPO with Exploration Bonuses ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> demonstrates that the adoption of exploration bonuses in the rearranged Lagrangian DPO objective effectively incentivizes exploration and expands the used preference data during training. When all problem parameters <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K,N^{\textup{CE}},M^{\textup{CE}},N^{\textup{on}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K,N^{\textup{CE}},M^{\textup{CE}},N^{\textup{on}}</annotation></semantics></math> are large enough, the suboptimality and constraint violation bounds will shrink to zero.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">While prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al.(2024)Huang, Li, Dobriban, Bastani, Hassani, and
Ding</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wachi et al.(2024)Wachi, Tran, Sato, Tanabe, and
Akimoto</a>]</cite> also provide theoretical results, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al.(2024)Huang, Li, Dobriban, Bastani, Hassani, and
Ding</a>]</cite> require an assumption that the optimal policy is feasible under the estimated reward and cost functions. The results in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wachi et al.(2024)Wachi, Tran, Sato, Tanabe, and
Akimoto</a>]</cite> have a term of the deviation between the used and optimal Lagrange multipliers, which can be unbounded since their algorithm does not contain any scheme to learn the optimal Lagrange multiplier. In addition, the results in prior works depend on preference data coverage. To the best of our knowledge, Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title='Theorem 2 (Result of Algorithm 𝙾⁢"-"⁢𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾). ‣ 5 Exploratory Primal-Dual DPO with Exploration Bonuses ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> is the first result for the constrained alignment problem (Eq. <a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) to get rid of the dependence on preference data coverage.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Experiments</h2>

<figure id="S6.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05703v1/combined_evaluation.svg" id="S6.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/135;" width="476" height="135"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>(Left) Rewards and negated costs of responses generated by compared language models when evaluated by Beaver-7b-unified-reward and Beaver-7b-unified-cost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>]</cite>. (Right) Helpfulness and harmlessness Elo scores evaluated by GPT-4. Our <math id="S6.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> model achieves the best helpfulness performance while reducing harmful content generation compared to the SFT model.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide experimental results to demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments are run on an Intel Xeon Platinum 8558 CPU and a single NVIDIA GH200 96GB GPU. Following prior works, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al.(2025)Kim, Jang, Kim, Kim, Lee, Bae, and
Lee</a>]</cite>, we use the PKU-SafeRLHF preference dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>]</cite> to train and evaluate models, and
take <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{Alpaca\mbox{-}7b\mbox{-}reproduced}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙰𝚕𝚙𝚊𝚌𝚊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟽</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚋</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚛𝚎𝚙𝚛𝚘𝚍𝚞𝚌𝚎𝚍</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{Alpaca\mbox{-}7b\mbox{-}reproduced}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Taori et al.(2023)Taori, Gulrajani, Zhang, Dubois, Li, Guestrin,
Liang, and Hashimoto</a>]</cite> as the SFT model, which is a fine-tuned version of the LLaMA-2-7b model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al.(2023b)Touvron, Martin, Stone, Albert,
Almahairi, Babaei, Bashlykov, Batra, Bhargava, Bhosale,
et al.</a>]</cite> on the Alpaca dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Taori et al.(2023)Taori, Gulrajani, Zhang, Dubois, Li, Guestrin,
Liang, and Hashimoto</a>]</cite>.
We compare our algorithm <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> with the SFT model <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{Alpaca\mbox{-}7b\mbox{-}reproduced}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙰𝚕𝚙𝚊𝚌𝚊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟽</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚋</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚛𝚎𝚙𝚛𝚘𝚍𝚞𝚌𝚎𝚍</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{Alpaca\mbox{-}7b\mbox{-}reproduced}</annotation></semantics></math> and existing open-source safety alignment algorithms <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{Beaver\mbox{-}v3.0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙱𝚎𝚊𝚟𝚎𝚛</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚟𝟹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathvariant="monospace">.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{Beaver\mbox{-}v3.0}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>]</cite> and <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{SafeDPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚂𝚊𝚏𝚎𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{SafeDPO}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al.(2025)Kim, Jang, Kim, Kim, Lee, Bae, and
Lee</a>]</cite>. Following existing works, we conduct both model-based and GPT-4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al.(2023)Achiam, Adler, Agarwal, Ahmad, Akkaya, Aleman,
Almeida, Altenschmidt, Altman, Anadkat, et al.</a>]</cite> evaluations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For model-based evaluation, the left sub-figure in Figure <a href="#S6.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the average reward and negated cost scores (to keep consistency between two sub-figures regarding harmlessness) of responses generated by compared language models, when evaluated by the reward model Beaver-7b-unified-reward and cost model Beaver-7b-unified-cost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>]</cite>. For GPT-4 evaluation, the right sub-figure in Figure <a href="#S6.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> plots the Elo scores of compared models regarding helpfulness and harmlessness evaluated by GPT-4. Figure <a href="#S6.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that our algorithm <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves the best performance regarding helpfulness while effectively improving harmlessness compared to the SFT model. While the harmlessness performance of our <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> is worse than <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{Beaver\mbox{-}v3.0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙱𝚎𝚊𝚟𝚎𝚛</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚟𝟹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathvariant="monospace">.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{Beaver\mbox{-}v3.0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{Beaver\mbox{-}v3.0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙱𝚎𝚊𝚟𝚎𝚛</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚟𝟹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathvariant="monospace">.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{Beaver\mbox{-}v3.0}</annotation></semantics></math> requires much higher memory and computational costs than our algorithm (cannot be run on a single GH200 GPU with 96GB memory), and does not have rigorous theoretical guarantees as our algorithm. This trade-off between performance and memory/computational cost is similar to the trade-offs between DPO and RLHF that have been reported in the literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al.(2023)Rafailov, Sharma, Mitchell, Manning, Ermon, and
Finn</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al.(2024)Xu, Fu, Gao, Ye, Liu, Mei, Wang, Yu, and Wu</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we study the constrained alignment problem for LLMs, which aims to maximize the reward while constraining the cost to stay below a threshold. We develop a novel primal-dual DPO approach for the offline and online data settings. Our approach adopts a rearranged Lagrangian DPO training objective, utilizing the reward information provided by a model trained using standard DPO. We establish suboptimality and constraint violation guarantees, and provide experimental results on the PKU-SafeRLHF dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>]</cite> to validate the effectiveness of our approach.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are several interesting directions for future work. One direction is to extend our theoretical results to the policy parameterization (large prompt-response space) setting. The challenge is that under popular policy parameterization settings such as the log-linear policy class, the constrained alignment problem can be non-convex with respect to the policy parameter. Another direction is to investigate stricter cost constraints, e.g., per-response constraints, which is challenging to tackle using neural networks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Ethics statement</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper studies the alignment of LLMs to enhance safety or impose certain constraints. The data used in experiments may contain harmful or offensive content.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Reproducibility statement</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper provides theoretical guarantees and experimental results for the proposed primal-dual DPO approach. All results are reproducible. For theoretical guarantees, the assumptions required are stated in Sections <a href="#S3" title="3 Preliminaries ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS2" title='4.2 A Provably Efficient Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A4" title='Appendix D Pseudo-code and Detailed Description of Algorithm 𝙾⁢"-"⁢𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>, and all proofs are presented in Appendix. For experimental results, the experimental setup is described in Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Experiments ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B More Experimental Details ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, and the code is provided in supplementary materials.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="Sx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The work was supported in part by NSF Grants CNS 23-12714 and CCF 22-07547 and AFOSR Grant, FA9550-24-1-0002. We also thank NVIDIA’s Applied Research Accelerator Program for donating two A100 GPUs to Prof. Rakesh Nagi’s group, which he graciously allowed us to use in the development stages of this work.</p>
</div>
</section>
