<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:173%;">Appendix<span id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
</div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>A Full Review of Related Work</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we give a more detailed review of related work.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With the extensive application of LLMs, the alignment of LLMs has received widespread attention in the AI community, which aims to make LLMs align with human preference and values, and become more helpful and harmless. RLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al.(2017)Christiano, Leike, Brown, Martic, Legg, and
Amodei</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al.(2022)Ouyang, Wu, Jiang, Almeida, Wainwright, Mishkin,
Zhang, Agarwal, Slama, Ray, et al.</a>]</cite> and DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al.(2023)Rafailov, Sharma, Mitchell, Manning, Ermon, and
Finn</a>]</cite> are two main algorithmic frameworks for LLM alignment. RLHF first trains a reward model, and then applies RL algorithms with the learned reward model to fine-tune LLMs. DPO directly fine-tune LLMs using preference data, without explicitly training a reward model.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While LLMs have achieved a remarkable success, they may also generate harmful and fabricated content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gehman et al.(2020)Gehman, Gururangan, Sap, Choi, and
Smith</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al.(2021)Lin, Hilton, and Evans</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al.(2023)Wei, Haghtalab, and Steinhardt</a>]</cite>. Recently, there are several works studying safety or constrained alignment of LLMs, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al.(2024b)Liu, Sun, and Zheng</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wachi et al.(2024)Wachi, Tran, Sato, Tanabe, and
Akimoto</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al.(2024)Huang, Li, Dobriban, Bastani, Hassani, and
Ding</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al.(2025)Zhang, Li, Hounie, Bastani, Ding, and
Ribeiro</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al.(2025)Kim, Jang, Kim, Kim, Lee, Bae, and
Lee</a>]</cite>.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>]</cite> proposed a safe RLHF framework, which considers maximizing the reward while restricting the cost to be no larger than a threshold. Their approach first trained a reward model and a cost model on reward and cost preference data, respectively, and then applied an RL algorithm, PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al.(2017)Schulman, Wolski, Dhariwal, Radford, and
Klimov</a>]</cite>, to maximize the Lagrangian function constituted by the learned reward and cost functions.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al.(2024b)Liu, Sun, and Zheng</a>]</cite> regenerated preference data according to the Bradley-Terry model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bradley &amp; Terry(1952)Bradley and Terry</a>]</cite> with the Lagrangian function using trained reward and cost models, and then performed the standard DPO algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al.(2023)Rafailov, Sharma, Mitchell, Manning, Ermon, and
Finn</a>]</cite> on these regenerated data. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wachi et al.(2024)Wachi, Tran, Sato, Tanabe, and
Akimoto</a>]</cite> observed a relationship between the optimal solution of maximizing the Lagrangian function and that of maximizing the reward function, and then applied DPO combined with this observation. The algorithm in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wachi et al.(2024)Wachi, Tran, Sato, Tanabe, and
Akimoto</a>]</cite> requires prior knowledge of the optimal Lagrangian multiplier, and their theoretical results depend on the deviation between the used and optimal Lagrange multipliers, which can be unbounded. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al.(2025)Kim, Jang, Kim, Kim, Lee, Bae, and
Lee</a>]</cite> reordered preference data if the preferred response (in terms of helpfulness) is unsafe and the dispreferred response is safe, and then ran DPO on these reordered data. Their algorithm is inefficient in cost information learning. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al.(2024)Huang, Li, Dobriban, Bastani, Hassani, and
Ding</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al.(2025)Zhang, Li, Hounie, Bastani, Ding, and
Ribeiro</a>]</cite> first learned the optimal Lagrangian multiplier, and then maximized the Lagrangian function under the learned optimal Lagrangian multiplier. These two works also require trained reward and cost models.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">There are also other works related to safety or constrained alignment of LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al.(2023)Zhou, Liu, Yang, Shao, Liu, Yue, Ouyang, and
Qiao</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al.(2024)Ji, Chen, Lou, Hong, Zhang, Pan, Qiu, Dai, and
Yang</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al.(2024)Yang, Liu, Xie, Huang, Zhang, and
Ananiadou</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al.(2025)Qi, Lyu, Yang, Wang, Bai, and Cui</a>]</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al.(2023)Zhou, Liu, Yang, Shao, Liu, Yue, Ouyang, and
Qiao</a>]</cite> designed a multi-objective DPO approach. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al.(2024)Ji, Chen, Lou, Hong, Zhang, Pan, Qiu, Dai, and
Yang</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al.(2024)Yang, Liu, Xie, Huang, Zhang, and
Ananiadou</a>]</cite> developed simple single-objective and multi-objective alignment approaches, respectively, but their algorithms need corrected responses or a multi-objective reward model.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al.(2025)Qi, Lyu, Yang, Wang, Bai, and Cui</a>]</cite> proposed a mixture of experts (MoE) framework for safety-helpfulness preference optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al.(2024b)Liu, Sun, and Zheng</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al.(2024)Huang, Li, Dobriban, Bastani, Hassani, and
Ding</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al.(2025)Zhang, Li, Hounie, Bastani, Ding, and
Ribeiro</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al.(2023)Zhou, Liu, Yang, Shao, Liu, Yue, Ouyang, and
Qiao</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al.(2024)Yang, Liu, Xie, Huang, Zhang, and
Ananiadou</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al.(2025)Qi, Lyu, Yang, Wang, Bai, and Cui</a>]</cite>, our approach avoids training reward and cost models, which significantly reduces memory and computational costs. In contrast to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wachi et al.(2024)Wachi, Tran, Sato, Tanabe, and
Akimoto</a>]</cite>, our approach does not need prior knowledge of the optimal Lagrangian multiplier. Moreover, most of the above works do not provide theoretical guarantees for their algorithms. By contrast, we establish rigorous suboptimality and constraint violation guarantees for the output policy of our algorithm, and remove the dependence on preference data coverage in the results in the extended online data setting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>More Experimental Details</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we will describe more details of algorithm implementation and experimental setup. Our code is written based on the released code of prior safe RLHF work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>]</cite> on their GitHub website, and we also open source our code in supplementary materials.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In algorithm implementation, we run our algorithm <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> (Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title='In 4.2 A Provably Efficient Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) without policy search constraints <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in\Pi^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in\Pi^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> in Line <a href="#S4.EGx19" title='In 1 ‣ 4.2 A Provably Efficient Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in\Pi^{c}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in\Pi^{c}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in Line <a href="#S4.EGx20" title='In 1 ‣ 4.2 A Provably Efficient Algorithm 𝙿𝙳⁢"-"⁢𝙳𝙿𝙾 ‣ 4 Primal-Dual DPO Utilizing Standard DPO ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, since these two constraints are mainly used for guaranteeing theoretical performance.
In experiments, we set the Lagrange multiplier of algorithm <math id="A2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> to save computational costs and time due to our limited computational resources. We find it works well in practice.
For algorithm <math id="A2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{SafeDPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚂𝚊𝚏𝚎𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{SafeDPO}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al.(2025)Kim, Jang, Kim, Kim, Lee, Bae, and
Lee</a>]</cite>, to guarantee fair comparison, we run it on the SFT model <math id="A2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{Alpaca\mbox{-}7b\mbox{-}reproduced}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙰𝚕𝚙𝚊𝚌𝚊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟽</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚋</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚛𝚎𝚙𝚛𝚘𝚍𝚞𝚌𝚎𝚍</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{Alpaca\mbox{-}7b\mbox{-}reproduced}</annotation></semantics></math>, without performing additional supervised fine-tuning on pairwise preference data as described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al.(2025)Kim, Jang, Kim, Kim, Lee, Bae, and
Lee</a>]</cite>.
In evaluation, we directly access the released models <math id="A2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{Alpaca\mbox{-}7b\mbox{-}reproduced}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙰𝚕𝚙𝚊𝚌𝚊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟽</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚋</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚛𝚎𝚙𝚛𝚘𝚍𝚞𝚌𝚎𝚍</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{Alpaca\mbox{-}7b\mbox{-}reproduced}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{Beaver\mbox{-}v3.0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙱𝚎𝚊𝚟𝚎𝚛</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚟𝟹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathvariant="monospace">.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{Beaver\mbox{-}v3.0}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>]</cite> via their Hugging Face websites.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The hyper-parameters of our algorithm used in experiments are presented in Table <a href="#A2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Appendix B More Experimental Details ‣ Primal-Dual Direct Preference Optimizationfor Constrained LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. For GPT-4 evaluation, we use the same prompts as in Appendix C.2 in prior safe RLHF work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>]</cite>. For the helpfulness measurement in GPT-4 evaluation, we use the helpfulness-based questions from the AlpacaEval dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al.(2023)Li, Zhang, Dubois, Taori, Gulrajani, Guestrin, Liang,
and Hashimoto</a>]</cite>. It is because as reported in the literature, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wachi et al.(2024)Wachi, Tran, Sato, Tanabe, and
Akimoto</a>]</cite>, GPT-4 tends to rate higher helpfulness scores for safer models, and thus using helpfulness-based questions rather than safety-sensitive questions in the PKU-SafeRLHF dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al.(2024)Dai, Pan, Sun, Ji, Xu, Liu, Wang, and Yang</a>]</cite> is more accurate for evaluating the helpfulness of models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Hyper-parameters of algorithm <math id="A2.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙿𝙳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝙳𝙿𝙾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{PD\mbox{-}DPO}</annotation></semantics></math> used in experiments.</figcaption>
